.TH "OSTRYA\-PUSH" "1" "" "ostrya" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ostrya\-push \- Push commits to a remote over ssh or HTTP, or to a bucket repository
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
ostrya push [OPTIONS] [REMOTE] [REFSPECS]...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Push commits to a remote over ssh or HTTP, or to a bucket repository.
.PP
The command resolves REMOTE and sends the objects that the server does not have. The server then updates all the refs of the refspecs in one transaction. Over ssh, the push runs the ssh client with the receive command on the remote side. Over HTTP, the push sends its requests to the receive endpoint of \fB\%ostrya serve\fR\&. To an \fB\%s3://\fR address, the push reads \fB\%[ex\-ostrya] keep\-detached\-related\fR from the \fB\%config\fR of the bucket, and with \fB\%false\fR drops \fB\%ostrya.related\fR\&.
.PP
Each DST and each tag names its namespace on the server: a plain NAME is a local ref, and \fB\%@ID:NAME\fR is a ref of the collection ID. The server writes the refs of its own collection id under \fB\%refs/heads/\fR, and the refs of each other collection under \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR\&. One push can write local refs and the refs of more than one collection. The bindings of the source commits select no namespace.
.PP
To an \fB\%s3://\fR address, the push writes a bucket repository in an \%S3 bucket with signed requests, and needs no server program. The bucket repository needs a \fB\%config\fR, which \fB\%remote init\fR writes. The push holds the writer lock of the bucket repository, uploads each object that the bucket does not hold, in archive form, and then writes the ref files. A commit object goes up after each object of its tree. The bucket gets the \fB\%\&.filez\fR bytes at the \fB\%[archive] zlib\-level\fR of this repository. The \fB\%config\fR of the bucket takes the role of the server: a plain DST, and \fB\%@ID:NAME\fR of its collection id, give \fB\%refs/heads/\fR, and each other collection gives \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR\&. The push writes no summary, and a summary of the bucket stays as it is. A pull of a ref that the summary lists takes the commit of the summary, so after the push moves that ref, the pull takes the old commit until \fB\%ostrya remote summary \-u\fR writes the summary again. The push refuses \%\-\-onto, \%\-\-tag, \%\-\-compress, a delete refspec, and the token and ssh options.
.PP
With \%\-\-onto, the push sends the tree of one local commit, and the server makes a new commit over it on the branch BRANCH. The server picks the parent, writes the bindings, and signs the commit.
.PP
The push also sends the dependencies that field \%2 of each pushed commit names, each with no parent and no ref, unless it takes \%\-\-onto.
.PP
With no refspec, the command reads the key \fB\%push\-branches\fR of the section of REMOTE, a list of ref patterns. It lists each local ref and each ref under \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR, and for each ref that the list selects, it sends the commit that the listing read to the ref of the same name: the local refs by name, then the collection refs by id and name. The listing reads only the ref files that the list selects. A remote ref is never sent. With \%\-\-collection=ID, the command keeps only the selected local refs and the selected refs of \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR, and drops each other selected ref with no message. If no ref matches, the command checks the options, the keys of the remote, and the environment as a push of a refspec checks them, up to the first file read, then writes a message to standard error and exits \%0. A refused value fails at exit \%1 with the error of a push of a refspec. The command does not read the token file, the TLS files, or the shared AWS files, and does not connect. The command refuses \%\-\-tag before it lists the refs. Without the key, with an address, or with \%\-\-onto, a refspec is required. \%ostrya\-config(1) states the key.
.PP
On success, standard output carries one line for each ref, in the order of the refspecs and then of the tags. A line holds the DST name, the old state, and the new state. The old state is a full commit checksum, \fB\%(new)\fR, or \fB\%(absent)\fR\&. The new state is a full commit checksum, \fB\%(unchanged)\fR, or \fB\%(deleted)\fR\&. With \%\-\-onto, standard output carries the checksum of the new commit alone.
.PP
Under \%\-\-verbose, one statistics line goes to standard error. If standard error is a terminal and \fB\%TERM\fR is set and is not \fB\%dumb\fR, standard error shows a progress bar while the push runs. A push to a bucket repository shows no bar while it lists the bucket and walks the commits. With \%\-\-break\-lock, a push that removed the writer lock of another writer writes one \fB\%warning: removed the writer lock of HOLDER\fR line to standard error. On failure, the command writes \fB\%error: MESSAGE\fR to standard error and nothing to standard output, and exits \%1.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIREMOTE\fR
The remote: a remote name, or an ssh, HTTP, or \fB\%s3://\fR address. Required.
.IP
A value with a \fB\%:\fR or a \fB\%/\fR is an address: \fB\%ssh://[USER@]HOST[:PORT]/PATH\fR, \fB\%[USER@]HOST:PATH\fR, \fB\%http://\fR, \fB\%https://\fR, or \fB\%s3://BUCKET[/PREFIX]\fR\&. Each other value names a \fB\%[remote "<name>"]\fR section in the config of this repository. The push address of the section is its \fB\%push\-url\fR key. If \fB\%push\-url\fR is absent, a \fB\%url\fR key with an \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR address is the push address. The \fB\%url\fR key holds no \fB\%s3://\fR address.
.IP
To an ssh address, the push runs the ssh client with the receive command on the remote side. To an HTTP address, the push sends its requests to the receive endpoint of \fB\%ostrya serve\fR\&. To an \fB\%s3://\fR address, the push writes the bucket repository at that address. A build without the feature \fB\%s3\fR refuses an \fB\%s3://\fR address.
.TP
\fIREFSPECS...\fR
The refspecs, each \fB\%SRC[:DST]\fR\&. At least one refspec is required, unless the section of REMOTE has the key \fB\%push\-branches\fR\&.
.IP
SRC is a revision of this repository, and DST is the ref of the server that takes its commit: a local ref NAME, or \%@ID:NAME, the ref NAME of the collection ID. If SRC is a ref, DST defaults to the text of SRC. A SRC that is a checksum, or that has a \fB\%^\fR suffix, needs a DST. \fB\%:DST\fR deletes the ref DST of the server.
.IP
DST is the last ref of the refspec. If the field before the last \fB\%:\fR starts with \fB\%@\fR, DST is \%@ID:NAME, else DST is the text after the last \fB\%:\fR\&. So \fB\%R:main:dst\fR sends the remote ref \fB\%R:main\fR to \fB\%dst\fR, \fB\%main:@X:a\fR sends \fB\%main\fR to \fB\%@X:a\fR, \fB\%@X:a:b\fR sends \fB\%@X:a\fR to \fB\%b\fR, and \fB\%:@X:a\fR deletes \fB\%@X:a\fR\&. A refspec with no \fB\%@\fR splits at its last \fB\%:\fR\&.
.IP
The command refuses a DST that is not NAME or \%@ID:NAME under the \%ref\-name rule, which includes a DST with \fB\%^\fR\&. It also refuses a plain DST of \%64 lowercase hex characters after a SRC, because a revision reads such a name as a commit checksum. It refuses a DST \%@OWN:NAME of that form after a SRC too, where OWN is the collection id of the server or of the bucket, before it sends an object. A delete of such a DST passes.
.IP
With \%\-\-onto, the operand is one REF, a revision of this repository.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
The number of parents of each source commit to send.
.IP
\%0 sends the commit alone, and \%\-1 sends the whole chain that this repository holds. The command refuses a value less than \%\-1.
.IP
Without this option, the push sends the chain back to the commit of the ref on the server. If the server does not have the ref, or the local chain does not have that commit, the push sends the source commit alone.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Update each ref whatever its state on the server.
.IP
The push also asks the server to allow an update that is not a \%fast\-forward. The server still refuses such an update unless the client group of the push has \fB\%w\fR and \fB\%d\fR on the ref.
.TP
\fB\-\-collection\fR \fIID\fR
Write each plain DST and each plain tag to the refs of the collection ID on the server.
.IP
A plain DST or tag NAME becomes \%@ID:NAME. A DST or tag \%@ID:NAME stays. The command refuses a DST or tag \%@Y:NAME with Y other than ID, also a DST that defaults to a SRC \%@Y:NAME. It refuses an ID that fails the \%collection\-id rule or is longer than \%255 bytes. Each refusal comes before the command starts the transport.
.IP
The server writes the refs of its own collection id under \fB\%refs/heads/\fR, and the refs of each other collection under \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR\&. The bindings of the source commits select no namespace.
.IP
With \%\-\-onto, the option applies to BRANCH and to each \%\-\-tag, and does not change \%\-\-bind\-ref.
.IP
With no refspec, the option keeps the local refs and the refs of \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/\fR that \fB\%push\-branches\fR selects, and drops each other selected ref.
.IP
To a bucket repository, the \fB\%config\fR of the bucket takes the role of the server: \%@ID:NAME of the collection id of the bucket writes \fB\%refs/heads/NAME\fR, and each other ID writes \fB\%refs/mirrors/ID/NAME\fR, also in a bucket with no collection id.
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\fR \fINAME\fR
Create the ref NAME on the server at the source commit.
.IP
NAME is a local ref NAME of the server, or \%@ID:NAME, the ref NAME of the collection ID. The option can occur more than once. A tag is a ref update that expects no ref, also with \%\-\-force. So a tag that exists gets \%ref\-mismatch after the server reports its refs, and before the push sends an object. The refspecs that are not deletes must give one commit, and the \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR of that commit must hold the name part of each tag: \fB\%v1\fR for \%@ID:v1. NAME passes the \%ref\-name rule, is not a remote ref, is not \%64 lowercase hex characters if it is plain, and is not a DST. The name part of a tag \%@OWN:NAME is not \%64 lowercase hex characters either, where OWN is the collection id of the server. The client group of the push needs \fB\%t\fR or \fB\%w\fR on the tag in its namespace. With no refspec on the command line, the command refuses the option.
.IP
With \%\-\-onto, the server creates the tag at the new commit, and the \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR of that commit holds its name part. The tag is not BRANCH. A tag that exists also gets \%ref\-mismatch before the push sends an object.
.TP
\fB\-\-disable\-verify\-bindings\fR
Do not check the \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR list of the commit of each DST and each tag.
.IP
Without this switch, the list must hold the name part of each DST and of each tag, or the push fails before it starts the transport. A commit with no \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR key passes for a DST. A server then applies the key \fB\%disable\-verify\-bindings\fR of the client group of the push, and without the key it refuses such a commit. A bucket repository has no server check, so the switch alone skips the check. The switch skips no check of \fB\%ostree.collection\-binding\fR\&. A ref that the push writes with the switch fails a pull without \%\-\-disable\-verify\-bindings. The switch does not change a push with \%\-\-onto.
.TP
\fB\-\-onto\fR \fIBRANCH\fR
Ask the server to commit the tree of REF onto the branch BRANCH.
.IP
The push sends the objects of the tree of the local commit that the server does not have. The server then makes a new commit over the tree, moves BRANCH to it, and creates each \%\-\-tag at it, in one transaction. The parent of the new commit is the commit of BRANCH on the server. The new commit takes the subject, the body, the metadata, and the timestamp of the local commit. The push leaves out the local \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR and \fB\%ostree.collection\-binding\fR, and the server writes the bindings of the namespace of BRANCH: the name parts of BRANCH and of each \%\-\-tag, and each \%\-\-bind\-ref. The push does not check the bindings of the local commit.
.IP
The push sends no parent, no commit object, and no detached metadata, so no signature of the local commit reaches the server. The server signs the new commit with the keys of its own client group.
.IP
The client group needs \fB\%c\fR on BRANCH, and \fB\%t\fR or \fB\%w\fR on each \%\-\-tag, each in its namespace. A server whose receive hooks take no server commit answers \%ref\-denied. The push sends the server commit in one frame, which an ostrya server limits to \%1 MiB. The reply names BRANCH and each \%\-\-tag, and must also fit in one frame. The push checks the two frames against the limit of the server before it sends an object.
.IP
BRANCH is a local ref NAME of the server, or \%@ID:NAME, the ref NAME of the collection ID. It passes the \%ref\-name rule, is not a remote ref, and is not \%64 lowercase hex characters if it is plain. The NAME of a BRANCH \%@OWN:NAME is not \%64 lowercase hex characters either, where OWN is the collection id of the server. BRANCH and each \%\-\-tag are in one namespace, after the server maps the refs of its own collection id to its local refs. The push refuses refs of two namespaces before it sends an object. With \%\-\-onto, the command takes exactly one REF, a revision of this repository, and does not split it at \fB\%:\fR\&. The local commit must not be partial, and must have no related objects. \%\-\-onto conflicts with \%\-\-depth and \%\-\-force.
.TP
\fB\-\-bind\-ref\fR \fINAME\fR
Bind the commit of \%\-\-onto to NAME, and create no ref.
.IP
The option can occur more than once. The \fB\%ostree.ref\-binding\fR of the new commit holds each NAME. NAME passes the \%ref\-name rule and has no \fB\%:\fR\&. The option needs \%\-\-onto.
.TP
\fB\-\-orphan\fR
Make the commit of \%\-\-onto with no parent.
.IP
If BRANCH exists on the server, the client group needs \fB\%c\fR and \fB\%d\fR on it, else the server answers \%non\-fast\-forward. The option needs \%\-\-onto.
.TP
\fB\-\-timestamp\fR \fITIMESTAMP\fR
Override the timestamp of the commit of \%\-\-onto.
.IP
The value is \fB\%@SECONDS\fR since the Unix epoch, or a date and time with a UTC offset (\fB\%2020\-01\-02T03:04:05Z\fR). Without this option, the new commit takes the timestamp of the local commit. The option needs \%\-\-onto.
.TP
\fB\-\-compress\fR[=\fILEVEL\fR]
Send the content objects deflated at LEVEL, \%1 to \%9.
.IP
The value needs the \fB\%=\fR form, and \%\-\-compress alone gives level \%6. Without this option, the push sends the content objects raw. With it, if this repository is an archive repository, the push sends each stored content object as it is, whatever LEVEL is. The object keeps the zlib level that the repository wrote it with.
.TP
\fB\-\-ssh\-command\fR \fICMD\fR
The ssh command, split at ASCII whitespace.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%OSTRYA_SSH_COMMAND\fR environment variable and over the \fB\%ssh\-command\fR key of the remote. Without each of the three, the push runs \fB\%ssh\fR\&. The split has no quoting rule. The command refuses an empty value, and refuses this option for an HTTP address.
.TP
\fB\-\-receive\-command\fR \fICMD\fR
The command that the remote side runs over ssh.
.IP
The remote shell parses the command. The push adds \fB\%\-\-repo=\fR and the path of the address in single quotes. This option wins over the \fB\%receive\-command\fR key of the remote. The default is \fB\%ostrya receive\fR\&. The command refuses an empty value, and refuses this option for an HTTP address.
.TP
\fB\-\-push\-token\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
The file whose first line is the token of an HTTP push.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR key of the remote. Without \%\-\-push\-user, the push sends the token as a bearer token. The command refuses a first line that is empty or longer than \%1 MiB. It refuses a token file for an \fB\%http://\fR address without \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials, and for an ssh address.
.TP
\fB\-\-push\-user\fR \fINAME\fR
Send the token as the password of a Basic credential with this name.
.IP
Without this option, the token is a bearer token. The option needs a token file, from \%\-\-push\-token\-file or from the \fB\%push\-token\-file\fR key of the remote. It wins over the \fB\%push\-user\fR key of the remote. The command refuses an empty name and a name that holds \fB\%:\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-client\-cert\-path\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM file of the client certificate of an HTTPS push.
.IP
It needs a key file: \%\-\-tls\-client\-key\-path or the \fB\%tls\-client\-key\-path\fR key of the remote. It wins over the \fB\%tls\-client\-cert\-path\fR key of the remote. A push to an \fB\%http://\fR address reads no TLS file, and the command refuses this option for an ssh address.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-client\-key\-path\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM file of the private key of the client certificate.
.IP
The key must need no passphrase. It needs a certificate file: \%\-\-tls\-client\-cert\-path or the \fB\%tls\-client\-cert\-path\fR key of the remote. It wins over the \fB\%tls\-client\-key\-path\fR key of the remote. The command refuses this option for an ssh address.
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-ca\-path\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM file of the CA certificates that verify the server.
.IP
With it, the push does not use the trust store of the host. It wins over the \fB\%tls\-ca\-path\fR key of the remote. A push to an \fB\%http://\fR address reads no TLS file, and the command refuses this option for an ssh address.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials\fR
Send the token to an \fB\%http://\fR address.
.IP
Without this switch, the command refuses a token to such an address before any request. Use it for a server on a loopback address or behind a proxy that terminates TLS. No key of the remote sets this switch. The command refuses it for an ssh address. For a \fB\%push\fR or a \fB\%push\-tree\fR to an \fB\%s3://\fR address, the switch permits an \fB\%http://\fR \%S3 server, to which the signature of each request goes in cleartext.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-region\fR \fIREGION\fR
The region of the signature of each request to the \%S3 server.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-region\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_REGION\fR and \fB\%AWS_DEFAULT_REGION\fR, and over the \fB\%region\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%us\-east\-1\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR
The \fB\%http://\fR or \fB\%https://\fR URL of the \%S3 server, with no path.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-endpoint\fR key of the remote, over \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL_S3\fR and \fB\%AWS_ENDPOINT_URL\fR, and over the endpoint of the AWS profile. Without each of them, the server is AWS. An \fB\%http://\fR URL needs \%\-\-allow\-cleartext\-credentials. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-addressing\fR \fISTYLE\fR
The addressing style of the bucket: \fB\%path\fR or \fB\%virtual\fR\&.
.IP
\fB\%path\fR puts the bucket in the path of each request, and \fB\%virtual\fR puts it in the host name. The option wins over the \fB\%s3\-addressing\fR key of the remote and over the \fB\%addressing_style\fR of the AWS profile. The default is \fB\%virtual\fR for AWS and \fB\%path\fR for a set server. A bucket name that holds a \fB\%\&.\fR gets \fB\%path\fR by default. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&. Possible values: path, virtual.
.TP
\fB\-\-s3\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
The profile of the shared AWS files.
.IP
It wins over the \fB\%s3\-profile\fR key of the remote and over \fB\%AWS_PROFILE\fR\&. A profile that this option or the key names skips the key pair, the region, and the endpoint of the environment. The default profile is \fB\%default\fR\&. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR\&.
.TP
\fB\-\-break\-lock\fR
Remove the writer lock of another writer of a bucket repository.
.IP
The push reads the lock, deletes it, and then takes its own lock. It writes the holder of the removed lock to standard error after a push that succeeds. Use it only when the holder is gone, for example after a crash: a writer whose lock is removed stops at its next check. The command refuses this option for an address that is not \fB\%s3://\fR, and with \%\-\-onto.
.PP
The global options are listed in \fBostrya\fR(1).
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBostrya\fR(1)
